Never infer cancellation from an AsyncSequence returning nil — that is the documented ambiguity, not a bug to work around. Two-line provable fix given this file's own structure: the ONLY way `ack` finishes without yielding is (a) the encode-throw path, already short-circuited at :53, or (b) cancellation. So make the child's fallthrough mean what it actually means:

    group.addTask {
        for try await _ in ack { return .acked }
        throw CancellationError()   // finished without a value == cancelled
    }

and add `if Task.isCancelled { return .queuedLocally }` immediately before `return first` at :91 so a cancellation that lands after the ack won is also downgraded. Delete the comment at :83. The general-purpose industrial pattern for 'callback API + deadline + cancellation' in Swift concurrency is `withTaskCancellationHandler(operation:onCancel:)` wrapping a continuation guarded by an atomic resumed-flag, with the deadline as a sibling `Task.sleep` — that is what Apple's own concurrency guidance and SE-0406's discussion of consumer cancellation point at. Whichever you pick, add the direct test the round is missing: `race` is `private`, so expose an `internal static func race` (or inject a `DocumentWriting` protocol) and drive all four mappings — encode-throw→.rejected, ack→.acked, deadline→.queuedLocally, cancellation→.queuedLocally. FirestoreAcked is the round's headline artifact and has zero executing tests.

Delete the branch and the comment. Then handle the real case where it belongs — client-side, not by parsing a server error code. The app already knows whether a document was ever uploaded: if the id was still in `pendingSyncIDs` when `remove()` ran, no ack was ever received, so the delete intent is satisfied and no tombstone should be created at all. `remove()` currently clears pending at :526 BEFORE the tombstone decision at :532, so capture it first:

    let wasNeverAcked = pendingSyncIDs.contains(drop.id)
    …
    guard cloud != nil, drop.receivedViaLink != true, !wasNeverAcked else { return }

That removes three wasted refusals, one spurious durable `unresolvedDeleteIDs` entry, and three round-trips per never-uploaded drop.

Separately, the correct general rule this branch was groping toward: classify Firestore write outcomes by gRPC status code, not by folklore. NOT_FOUND, PERMISSION_DENIED, UNAUTHENTICATED, INVALID_ARGUMENT and FAILED_PRECONDITION are terminal; UNAVAILABLE, DEADLINE_EXCEEDED, ABORTED, RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED and INTERNAL are retryable. Building that taxonomy once, in FirebaseDropStore, is also the fix for state-3 — do it once and both findings close.

Classify terminality from the SERVER's status code, which is the only authority that can issue it. In `FirebaseDropStore.save`, unwrap `.rejected(let error as NSError)` and map `error.domain == FirestoreErrorDomain` with the non-retryable gRPC codes — PERMISSION_DENIED (7), UNAUTHENTICATED (16), INVALID_ARGUMENT (3), FAILED_PRECONDITION (9), NOT_FOUND (5) — to a new `DropStoreError.refusedByServer(String)`. Keep the ownerUid pre-check purely as a cheap fast path. In DropStore.swift add a catch beside :618:

    catch DropStoreError.refusedByServer(let reason) {
        guard syncGenerations[drop.id] == generation else { return }
        clearPending(drop.id); markTerminal(drop.id)
        syncFailureReasons[drop.id] = reason
    }

This is the standard retryable/terminal taxonomy every durable-write client implements (gRPC status-code semantics; Google's own client libraries retry exactly UNAVAILABLE / DEADLINE_EXCEEDED / ABORTED / RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED / INTERNAL and fail fast on the rest). It simultaneously stops the infinite re-push loop.

Independently, PERSIST ownerUid: after a successful `cloud.save`, write the stamped uid back through `local.save` so the client pre-check has durable input. That single change is also the missing input for state-6's ownership predicate and for flows-5's conditional revalidation, so it unblocks three findings at once.

Until either lands, ShareDropSheet must not render the reassuring caption at :147 for a drop with a non-nil `syncFailureReasons[drop.id]` — the state lens is right that a recorded reason with no surface is the shape of the bug.

Treat acknowledgment as a mutator, not a bystander — it changes user-visible state, so it takes the same generation discipline every other mutator already has:

    func acknowledgeReadIncident() {
        refreshGeneration &+= 1          // supersede any in-flight refresh
        localReadWarning = nil           // synchronous; the method is already @MainActor
        Task { [weak self] in await self?.localDiagnostics?.acknowledgeIncident() }
    }

That is textbook optimistic UI: mutate the published state on the isolated actor synchronously, do the durable write asynchronously, and drop the equality guard on the async tail because nothing after the await touches published state. Then make `refresh` respect the acknowledgment — track `acknowledgedIncidentGeneration` and skip re-posting a warning whose text was acknowledged after the load that produced it.

Invert the write order as well: clear the visible state first and the journal last, so a failed journal write leaves the banner up (safe, user can retry) rather than the reverse (banner gone, incident record gone, evidence lost).

And take the scoping point flows-6 makes: Dismiss must acknowledge a NOTIFICATION, never resolve the underlying STATE. Once `unresolvedDeleteIDs` and the unaccounted-pending count have their own surfaces (ux-3, ux-4), dismissing the corruption banner no longer removes the last trace of unfinished work.
